{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target answers the wrong question. The problem requires identifying what evidence *within a single evaluation* would distinguish a genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance that happens to track the right answer. SRC_001 instead argues a substantive claim about black-swan anomalies and offers two counterconditions describing when its claim would NOT hold. Neither the mechanism nor the counterconditions supplies the requested discriminating-evidence criterion. The counterconditions are scope-of-claim exclusions ('outcomes entirely contained within checklist parameters'; 'panel encouraged to report out-of-bounds observations'), not intra-evaluation evidentiary tests that separate true reliability from coincidentally-correct compliance. The target thus never engages the distinguishing-evidence requirement that defines the problem.","counterexample":["A target that proposed, e.g., 'within one evaluation, insert adversarial items where the checklist cues point to the wrong answer; if the panel still follows the checklist to the wrong answer while the expert gets them right, compliance is tracking cues not reliability; if the panel departs from checklist cues and matches the expert, the gain is genuine' would satisfy the asked question."],"target":"378ffd0db54ec1612f2296787fc63f49403ec6dadb97546c0c1c41c65ec11d8e"}]}